course: dictionary
lesson: 4765
title: Words from long ago
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to the past. You may not use them today, but authors still use them in old stories. «The housemaid lit the fires before anyone woke up.» The clues are lit, fires and the early start: a housemaid was someone who did jobs inside a house.
explain: The word housemaid is made of two smaller words, house and maid. A maid is a girl or woman servant, so put the parts together and you get the job: a servant who works in a house. Splitting a word into smaller parts is a useful trick for working out new words.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Alice says, «He took me for his housemaid», so the Rabbit thought she was a servant. If you put "servant who cleaned the house" in place of housemaid, the sentence still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: A housemaid is a woman or girl servant who cleaned and tidied a house in the past.
fact: Housemaids also made beds and lit fires.
fact: «Housemaid» is a noun: one housemaid, several housemaids.
fact: In Victorian and Edwardian times, many young women worked as housemaids in big houses, for low pay and long hours.
fact: In Alice in Wonderland, the White Rabbit mistakes Alice for his housemaid, Mary Ann, and sends her to fetch his gloves and fan.
exercise: The White Rabbit mistakes Alice for someone. Who does he think she is? | The Duchess's cook | *His housemaid Mary Ann | The Frog-Footman | His gardener Pat
exercise: «Many middle-class Victorian families had at least one servant, such as a housemaid, a cook or a gardener.» What did a housemaid do? | Sold tickets on a train | Drove the carriages to town | *Cleaned, tidied and made beds | Painted the outside walls of the castle
exercise: The Rabbit orders her to fetch «a pair of gloves and a fan». What did he want her to fetch? | A cup of tea and a biscuit | *A pair of gloves and a fan | His lost book and a candle | A ball and a hoop
test: In Victorian times, what did many young women who worked as housemaids get? | Very high pay and short hours | *Low pay and long hours | Gold coins and a big house of their own | A holiday by the sea every week
test: Which two smaller words make up housemaid? | horse and maid | hose and maid | house and made | *house and maid
exam: «He took me for his housemaid», Alice says. What does housemaid mean here? | A cat who lived in the house | A girl who went to school | A visitor who came to stay | *A servant who cleaned and tidied a house
